	<key>readme</key>
	<string>Search your Chrome browsing history from Alfred using Kwel.

Kwel for Chrome: https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/kwel/mhablbmgafbdofgaffhdnnknjhafmlin

1. Download Kwel.alfredworkflow from https://github.com/Masa-Ryu/kwel-connect/releases and double-click it to import into Alfred.
2. Enable Local Connection (Alfred Integration in older versions) in Kwel Settings and copy the Profile ID.
3. Paste the ID into Kwel Profile ID below.
4. Add Search Kwel in Alfred Preferences → Features → Default Results → Setup fallback searches and save.

Open Alfred, type Kyoto, choose Search Kwel, and press Return. Select a page and press Return to open it in Chrome.

The first Search Kwel invocation installs or reuses the shared Kwel Connect Host. Keep Chrome open; results appear automatically when Kwel connects. If it remains disconnected, check Local Connection and the Profile ID in the same Chrome profile.

## macOS First Launch

Kwel Connect for macOS is currently unsigned and not notarized. macOS may block `kwel-alfred`, `kwel-bridge`, or `kwel-native-host` on first launch. Each executable may require a separate confirmation.

Before proceeding, verify that the files came from the official GitHub Releases (https://github.com/Masa-Ryu/kwel-connect/releases) and match the published SHA-256 checksums.

For an unidentified-developer or notarization warning, after trying to open the executable, you can decide whether to allow that executable in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Open Anyway. If the warning reports malware or says the software will damage your computer, do not approve it; check the exact warning text.

Do not disable macOS security protections.

See [Apple's official support instructions](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102445).</string>
